{
 "interiors": {
  "_about": "(0.4.4) How every interior of ShadowVeil is fitted out (rpg.world.shadowveil.Fitout). 'programs': per building purpose, the rooms of each storey - 'single' for a one-storey building, else 'ground', 'upper' (every storey between) and 'top' (the highest of three or more) - each room a use and its share of the storey; the first room is the one the way in opens into, the rest lie behind it, walled off where both are big enough ('walls': false keeps them one open room), hung with a door where 'door' says ('plain' or 'locked'). A courtyard's ranges take 'volumes' in turn, biggest first. 'beds' is the fewest beds the building has (its census residents can ask for more); 'bedsBySize' a bed for every so many square cells of footprint where nobody lives yet. 'wealth' (poor, common, rich, royal) picks the furniture a piece's 'wealth' list allows. 'uses': per room use, its pieces in the order they matter - place 'wall' (backed onto a wall; 'corner' wants two), 'bed' (head to a wall; 'beds': the room's share of the building's beds), 'centre' (a table and its 'seats'), 'counter' (across the room: staff behind, customers before; its 'post' and posts), 'rows' (pews), 'altar', 'cells' (a jail's), 'stalls', 'hang' (on a wall), 'rug', 'ceiling'; 'station' (a Recipes.Station) or 'fitting' (chest) instead of a 'kind'; 'trades' sets the stations the census workers' callings need. 'need': a defect if it cannot be placed; 'chance': how likely; 'post': where the building's people stand to work. Every room is lit (its pieces, else candles, else a chandelier).",
